=== the 403 that was the chat's own fetch tool =======================
$ aws s3 ls s3://aws-devops-sdet-demo-site-993912191738/cost/ --recursive
2026-08-09 05:43:49  19105 cost/stage/31296973693-destroy.json
2026-08-09 05:43:50  19105 cost/stage/latest.json

$ for u in cost/stage/latest.json cost/stage/31296973693-destroy.json \
           timeline/stage/latest.json; do
    printf '%s  %s\n' "$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' \
      "https://demo.uveapp.net/$u")" "$u"; done
200  cost/stage/latest.json
200  cost/stage/31296973693-destroy.json
200  timeline/stage/latest.json

The object existed, was public, and answered 200 to two clients. The 403 came
from the chat session's own fetcher and from nothing else.

=== the teardown, against AWS rather than against Terraform state ====
$ ( set -e
    P=(--profile demo-admin --region us-west-2)
    acct=$(aws sts get-caller-identity "${P[@]}" --query Account --output text)
    ecr=$(aws ecr describe-repositories "${P[@]}" ...)
    ecs=$(aws ecs list-clusters "${P[@]}" ...)
    rds=$(aws rds describe-db-instances "${P[@]}" ...)
    alb=$(aws elbv2 describe-load-balancers "${P[@]}" ...)
    nat=$(aws ec2 describe-nat-gateways "${P[@]}" ...)
    eks=$(aws eks list-clusters "${P[@]}" ...)
    ... )
account 993912191738
CONTROL ecr aws-devops-sdet-demo-app
ecs
rds
alb
nat
eks

Five empty readings, and they are evidence only because the sixth is not: the
control sits on a permanent level in the same block, under `set -e`, so an
expired token aborts instead of printing six clean-looking blanks.
